#{[#edb/ref 17592186050574 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069567} :activity/id #uuid "09c8fe50-cab2-5dfd-a90a-5053ffffc1bf" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069958 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068547 :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3567" :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, continuous at $x=0?$\n\n![](images/e3/e395863ca66a7e87fa335c56e5b6aeb1ec206893e57b155bf64e5ab07be7545c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068548 :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3568" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x),$ plotted below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d0d3c2cd072841546e66d70105436200e316e156901f8fe27dc150e96ff3da4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2.5.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these three points without taking our pencil off of the page."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068549 :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\n- $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069961 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" :tutorial/title "Continuity at a Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069568}}] :activity/title "Determining Continuity from Graphs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068989}] [#edb/ref 17592186049660 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088728} :activity/id #uuid "21e3df28-881c-51eb-8c4e-abefad2477c8" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088868 :tutorial/content "Adding a value to $y=2^x$ will **translate** (shift) the exponential function up or down the $y$-axis. \n\nFor instance, adding $3$ to $y=2^x$ will move each point on the exponential function $3$ units up.\n\n![](images/c8/c89fd5fdcdc3773143a5f14ecb20f5bd2917bce20ece18a7dc70631bf358a0e8.png)\n\nNote the following points:\n\n- The $y$-intercept $(0,1)$ has moved up $3$ units to the point $(0,4).$\n\n- The horizontal asymptote $y=0$ has moved up $3$ units to $y=3.$\n\nAs a second example, let's consider the function $y=2^x-3.$ In this case, the graph of $y=2^x$ is shifted *down* by $3$ units.\n\n![](images/84/84987b3e9138a2e7e8afe70a7aa28a1cd81e081033aa2a766773e63cb8f41da5.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088729}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087425 :question/id #uuid "8ab1df94-0c4f-5720-a9b6-939dba0ab9c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1219" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=4^{x}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=4^{x}{:}$\n\n![](images/df/dfac014ecedaa03a46048b4729ddfa60e7a3776322d0b9ea19035fbcd24bf85b.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y=4^x-1,$ we take the graph of $y=4^x$ and shift it $1$ unit down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\n![](images/ee/eef6c5dd67c683ccd0826caa9760fa240be973f8debd29b41d463f6184a00620.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087426 :question/id #uuid "65c814f8-df2f-549d-9467-c6dda6890bb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1220" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y=3^{x}+c$ . What is the value of $c?$\n\n![](images/00/00264eb90dbaa2c50fdd626d61292c0428fd0bbbd03a6d051353598450b4ad89.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y=3^x$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y=3^x+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Therefore, $c=2.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088731}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088869 :tutorial/content "When an exponential growth function is vertically shifted *and* stretched, we apply the stretch first and the shift second.\n\nFor example, to plot the function $y=2(4^x)+3,$ we start by plotting the graph of $y=2(4^x).$ Stretching $y=4^x$ by a factor of $2$ moves the $y$-intercept from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/5d/5d6bdd7aa3290567fc6a64747ea605970fcb1d0f05ba06907134e7fc0a3e290b.png)\n\nThen, to plot the graph of $y=2(4^x)+3,$ we take the graph of $y=2(4^x)$ and shift it $3$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=3.$\n\n![](images/19/191a51b0b4b7da7c6744240f36786d55b8b525356b8d0300750fb48af5a2f06a.png)" :tutorial/title "Combining Vertical Shift and Vertical Stretch"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088732}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087427 :question/id #uuid "f73207f4-4661-55f1-acfb-cc5fe6ba9316" :question/math-academy-id "e-1221" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=3^{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1c/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c92ea5f3cbfd40bd0dc384b152f6166c27400dbedd93a9c8333195950e7f9c3.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8e8cdccd58a9f1f21c8c2bf0a4a8c08e9c263c4fe91cc92e2bee334de057116.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088733}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087428 :question/id #uuid "62f7d6e1-61ec-5f3d-8cdd-4da0c90c7ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-1222" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k(6^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/de/deb7e20658882229a3653f4058c787bf2a5c4a26c5f2982406a2dba34c2d5846.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k(6^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4$, so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({6}^{x})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k({6}^{0})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 6 & =k+4 \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2(6^{x})+4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}}] :activity/title "Vertical Translations of Exponential Growth Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087901}] [#edb/ref 17592186049731 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125322} :activity/id #uuid "7d6120fd-aa08-5562-9120-ce173e8fe311" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125752 :tutorial/content "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124283 :question/id #uuid "454d081a-fdcb-5101-9f85-0e3b27986695" :question/math-academy-id "e-3961" :question/problem "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\n![](images/6f/6fbded3e0a3faefda0e1f2c8418f5e271fb116cde4082487096378f89511b695.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n![](images/50/5036479a948abee5644cd3b96632f6aaf8ee370f7473ba27e11d25bb84ddb781.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- In graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV  shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125324}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124284 :question/id #uuid "b6722faa-6069-59c1-8861-75b8207b0a90" :question/math-academy-id "e-3962" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d965e0b1087ad45206dd9259c963609f556bf5803af47ab18006bf40dffb2557.png)\n\n     \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $(-3,-1]$\n\n- $(0,4]$\n\n- $[-2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.   \n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,-1].$  Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/24/245484a6a77160a5f5892738a7e1f8e488f6df8e4ba990bbc94ae964c3305525.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $(0,4]$ and $[-2,2)$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/47/4737a1460e1abd4edc200404407fef10761f038d9a29e52ba0f8c3e8c69bb93f.png)\n\n          \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125325}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125325 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124285 :question/id #uuid "7c28a136-0b86-5d13-a5f8-b0dd0e0ea1ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-3963" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n\n- $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n\n- $x \\in (-1,4]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:  \n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y= |x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y= |x-2|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-2|-2.$\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2].$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4].$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}}] :activity/title "Invertible Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124694}] [#edb/ref 17592186048219 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130064} :activity/id #uuid "24e4f3d3-57f6-51a9-afd6-0c3e9a2f8f97" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130392 :tutorial/content "We have explored linear and affine transformations in the plane. We'll now begin to explore nonlinear transformations of plane regions. \n\nLet  $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a **transformation** on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ We write\n![](images/11/1137221ffc72c194aba941eaee899cc0a4134a92e92e86cfa65c88acd5f702ec.svg) \n\nRecall that, under this notation, \n\n- ![](images/0d/0decb326ae66f97fb75a8df874479061fb1557b0437ca8ea1e7c5ed645db4b19.svg) represents vectors belonging to the input space (i.e., the $uv$-plane), and\n\n- ![](images/09/091b7a3ddf6ad7dc35360e728c2946481b3a17f60525d09676eb469ebd518a4d.svg) represents vectors belonging to the output space (i.e., the $xy$-plane).\n\nFor example, consider the following transformation:\n\n![](images/23/239781240f2def3a5adf5331f36fa6dbdd3657ab431195c30cf3ede49d2ef3e2.svg) \n\nGeometrically, this transformation maps the rectangular region in the $uv$-plane below to a circular sector in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc80fe1271d560e9f958eba9454761d56f936567dc439853bc4181cccc3f76f.png)\n\nWe'll explore how to compute the image of a nonlinear transformation in future lessons. But for now, it is sufficient to know that \n\n- $\\mathbf T$ takes points (or vectors) from the $uv$-plane as input and maps them to points (vectors) in the $xy$-plane, and\n\n- rectangular regions in the $uv$-plane are mapped to regions in the $xy$-plane that are not necessarily parallelograms." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130225}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129006 :question/id #uuid "28562339-0fe0-5d56-8767-2ec0834525d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6810" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/eb/eb8cb887e2c6e273a9a59bdbffeb0e8a2af4f8dcf5cc376ba4318128201ef936.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/92/928c9f7761e597884525a92388346c486c4e0df9c2c942cf31ad87164676613e.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130202}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128838 :question/id #uuid "eec994b4-82ac-568c-b493-dc9a21102c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-11156" :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/7f/7fc9eb28594744f04c1934d10b7e9751c9f9855897f4cc4da05b8a5e82a93f3d.svg)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](images/5e/5e056d63a91b0f4ae7dcb1c076764b862b0321b47c6e586cc91acd9e01dd36c8.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/37/370aba8042cc3dbd6c5c9dc15751e50dd8fe7be36d0cb220966da075bf9e2e43.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](images/1c/1ca433e68fd2a57e9bd9666b76c0577fd84621eededde3a50d03a49723b21739.svg) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130226}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130225 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130460 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n![](images/f9/f9a1c3375eed570284c4e001f119f21901f4493a0b851919b92b678a74187444.svg) \n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that  $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$ \n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n![](images/b4/b433bcafea1c84c67f2fb65ac8343a4d8401ce6d4e745f6d464b010a4258164a.svg) \n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =u{cos}^{2}⁡v+u{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{sin}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." :tutorial/title "The Jacobian Determinant"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130226 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130461 :tutorial/content "To understand what the Jacobian describes, we recall our knowledge of affine transformations.\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the matrix equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = T\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf b,$ and $\\mathbf x$ are vectors from $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nFor an affine transformation, the determinant of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n- The *area scale factor* of the transformation:\n\nIf a rectangle in the $uv$-plane has area $A,$ its image in the $xy$-plane is a parallelogram of area $|\\det(T)|\\cdot A.$\n\n![](images/04/04f36d98bf7589d1faadeffee0812590337fd36d8bb44f5abe1920948dee5bb0.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *orientation-preserving* or *orientation-reversing:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) > 0,$ the transformation is orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/49/496a49ee541eab31f69f9c571e450547b0c6942b100bc8e24fbd927dd43e4d33.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) < 0,$ the transformation is orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/21/211369d6f3fc70bcf7cf40f3270c24c245d306247efc429cb85589e42c19ee07.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *invertible* or *non-invertible:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is invertible.\n\n![](images/e4/e4b840ad5a3efa082ac4056d1d9b7c03d2e3e11e016e053df055dbb2b39f0d1f.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) = 0,$ the transformation is non-invertible. In such cases, a rectangle in the $uv$-plane collapses to either a line segment or point in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9a038c5de0e9f3205194fd7941a4b371c695c51c294f2ad5ac0b8fc1015811d1.png)\n\n![](images/98/9813ba294a0242abbfb885ebd3e82505d05a2f8a1cfa3975c7859f9530cac8b1.png)\n\nAffine transformations are special because these properties are *global*. In other words, they apply *everywhere* in the plane. So, for example, if $\\textrm{det}(T) = 2$, then the image of *every* rectangle in the $uv$-plane is a parallelogram with double the area of its preimage." :tutorial/title "A Review of Affine Transformations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130227}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130462 :tutorial/content "The Jacobian determinant gives information about nonlinear transformations similar to that described by the determinant properties of affine transformations.\n\nThe critical difference is that, in general, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones. \n\nTo understand what this means, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation, where\n![](images/d0/d0149013f12e5cdad31611d4c64489c0aaf6049707e9eed22202ae70d2d8a38a.svg) \n\nand we assume that $x(u,v)$ and $y(u,v)$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on $U.$\n\nFor ease of notation, let's introduce $J(u_0,v_0)$ to denote the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0){:}$\n\n$$\nJ(u_0,v_0) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nLet's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangle in the $uv$-plane whose bottom-left corner is the point $(u_0,v_0).$\n\n![](images/dc/dc084ec02cd0c4fac71379590e8dc4973354cacc898e267a0eb15a6e8500f31f.png)\n\nIt's important to note that the image of our rectangle in the $xy$-plane is typically *not* a parallelogram! However, provided that our rectangle is very small, its image can be *approximated* by a parallelogram (we'll justify this in a future lesson). \n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangle and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The *local* area scale factor of $\\mathbf T$. \n\nSuppose the length and width of our rectangle are $\\delta u$ and $\\delta v,$ so its area is $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v.$ Then, it can be shown that the parallelogram in the $xy$-plane has an area equal to $\\left|J(u_0,v_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta A.$\n\n![](images/be/be1771ebb041c418c58d7d5615510e2fcacfeaf3677fbace739498ef1998ef11.png)\n\nNote the similarity of this formula with that of affine transformations described earlier (we've simply replaced $\\det(T)$ with the Jacobian).\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)> 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/8c/8c649fdd526a831fb990393b292fb4582dad14c2391d129e9ed59792ebb8ac31.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)<  0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/ae/ae5a3d0a42e1c4b9f692994b89815382a30cdf3e676bbd9844258ad2a6961608.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)\\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\n![](images/33/33ca98206a347e13aa8ec179a52dd755008d9a7d74882391e0c3f2344f6f6641.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\n![](images/3d/3d4534c263a9a5bb26ba35ce8f879cf01db741441d948c023acaa9407d58e6e0.png)\n\n![](images/65/659f26ac6aba808d66a760d5683f265fd7c54431d894b2eabdc255b46368fc24.png)\n\nPoints $(u_0,v_0)$ such that $J(u_0,v_0)=0$ are called **critical points.** Critical points are very important. We'll discuss these in more detail in future lessons.\n\nWe again stress that the Jacobian describes *local* properties, that is, local to the point $(u_0,v_0).$ The effect of a nonlinear transformation on an object varies as we move around the $uv$-plane, so its local properties also vary. So, for example, if a transformation is orientation-preserving at one point, it does *not* mean it's orientation-preserving everywhere!" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Jacobian"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130230}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128860 :question/id #uuid "32f92fce-b4b9-5e7a-9fe0-255b15edf312" :question/math-academy-id "e-12417" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}.$ Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n![](images/ff/ff668919204d7ccd57c1711fed961ca73562d47cc6f100511b06fe54a5ea212d.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\big(\\mathbf{T}(\\Delta)\\big) \\approx \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{Area}(\\Delta)}_{\\large \\delta{A}}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{3} & 3u{v}^{2} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{3}\\cdot 2v-3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,2)} & =(2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{4}-6(-1)^{2}(2)^{2} \\\\  & =32-24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|8|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =8\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128861 :question/id #uuid "6102567f-7035-5fa2-b12e-80a0470de4a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12418" :question/problem "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](images/57/57874e6e685ee9fb1a2b527fe046696aa983b04d22751c72d8a76783b349ca18.svg)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  <    0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n![](images/45/4595adcbeb5ee3dc0bbe1258536e9b0e3e2fc81d5720a3394029df814f55f949.svg)\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & u{e}^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{v}\\cdot (-2v)-u{e}^{v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v) & <0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/2b/2b6128c5a1737e3ad1fd82f91f5ccd320421ad3472ae8152440034ecea10d364.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}}] :activity/title "The Jacobian" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129344}]}
